Model Answer:
A student has a right of appeal to the Appeal Committee against Board of Discipline (BOD) sanctions only if the student has been:

(a) expelled from the University;  
(b) suspended or excluded from the University or any part of it or from any programme or courses or excluded from any examination;  
(c) fined more than $5,000; or  
(d) deprived of the award of degree, diploma or certificate or other academic distinction to be awarded or previously awarded.  

For other sanctions imposed by the BOD, the student does not have a right of appeal to the Appeal Committee. The Appeal Committee hears appeals against decisions or sanctions by the BOD and its decision is final [SOURCE 1].

Plagiarism is defined in the Academic Integrity document as using or passing off one’s own writings or ideas of another without acknowledging or crediting the source from which the ideas are taken. It includes:
- The use of words, images, diagrams, graphs, or ideas derived from books, journals, magazines, visual media, and the internet without proper acknowledgement;
- Copying of work from the internet or any other sources and presenting it as one’s own;
- Submitting identical work for different courses or to different journals and publications.

Students are expected to observe academic integrity by providing references when citations are mentioned in their work [SOURCE 2].
